id: design.observability
kind: design
title: Observability
aliases: observability, metrics logs traces, monitoring, telemetry
tags: operations, diagnostics
questions:
  - what would you instrument in a new service?
invariant: Metrics answer "how much"; logs answer "what happened once"; traces answer "where the time went". Each has its own cost model.
answer_30s: Instrument four signals -- request rate, errors, duration, saturation (RED plus saturation) -- plus one correlation id across logs and traces, and alert on symptoms users feel rather than on causes.
answer_2m: |
  The discipline is choosing the right instrument: metrics are aggregated and
  cheap but lose detail (counters and histograms, low cardinality -- never a
  user id as a label), logs are detailed and expensive (structured, sampled,
  never secrets), traces show causal chains across services (sampled, and
  useful above all for tail latency). Coverage that matters for a service: RED
  per route, saturation per resource (queue depth, pool waits, in-flight
  concurrency), dependency health (latency and error rate per upstream), and
  business counters. Alert on symptoms (error budget burn, p99 latency, queue
  depth) rather than on every cause, and make sure every alert has a runbook
  entry -- an alert nobody can act on trains people to ignore alerts.
  Distributed tracing requires context propagation: without a propagated id the
  three pillars cannot be joined, and correlation is where most of the value
  actually lives.
when_to_use:
  - every service, worker and controller; decide at design time, not during the first incident
failure_modes:
  - high-cardinality labels, so the metrics backend becomes the outage
  - logs without structured fields or ids, so incidents are reconstructed by grep
  - alerting on causes with no action, so the signal is drowned
  - tracing without propagated context, so traces end at the first service boundary
related:
  - design.health-checking
  - design.timeouts
  - rust.tracing
  - k8s.logs
  - k8s.top-resource-usage
  - recipe.incident-diagnosis
sources:
  - https://sre.google/sre-book/monitoring-distributed-systems/
